{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey municipal rent control framework","requirement":"New Jersey has no statewide law establishing, governing or controlling rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances enforced by locally created boards, and rent increases must comply with those ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in municipalities that adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances; a tenant may ask the local Rent Control Board or municipal clerk whether a unit is covered.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Units covered by municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt; HUD-assisted or HUD-insured developments and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Landlord/Tenant Information (Rent Control/Rent Increases)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"State law sets no rent cap; locally created boards enforce municipal rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs for 30 years after construction of the building is completed.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings within 30 years of completion"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered new buildings from local rent control ordinances for 30 years.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise the rent mid-lease term; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease with the increased rent. Notice to quit and notice of rent increase are required before evicting for nonpayment of an increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; any increase must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units covered by the Anti-Eviction Act"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"The increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Rents may be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling unit only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause. A Notice to Quit is required except for nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units; all residential landlords, whether the lease is written or oral.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer, return and withholding penalties","requirement":"On sale or transfer, the new owner must obtain the security deposit plus accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for its investment, notices, interest and return. Willfully withholding deposits of tenants receiving government assistance is penalized.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; the special penalty applies where the tenant received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense for willfully and intentionally withholding deposits of assisted tenants, collected by summary proceeding; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense, fine of not less than $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Credit report and rental application fee charges","requirement":"A landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of a credit or screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification from applicants.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applicants for residential rental units in New Jersey; landlords may use credit reports in deciding whether to approve or deny an applicant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Prospective residential tenants"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Landlord/Tenant Information (Credit Checks and Background Checks)","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.65,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source of lawful income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not discriminate in the rental of housing, including on the basis of source of lawful income, and must give equal treatment in the rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home, single-sex residences, and age-restricted housing as it pertains to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All landlord-tenant relationships except listed owner-occupied and single-sex housing"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, such as the YMCA; age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD landlords who violate the law face fines of up to $10,000 for a first offense and may have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
